[{:db/id "q-126521/selection/03b2af20ed1e2ea358d70c5f4f3b7e7bfd35970c4c6194648dba88c96215de87" :answer/id #uuid "b8687539-ed01-557c-83f6-141a2ad1f379" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126521/selection/1ea68ea8aca2efd79850eb2489b3463c6afabdb3cd1b94855d0dbf435a934118" :answer/id #uuid "6ce72f66-28c7-5cea-8e12-eaec17c94469" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126521/selection/5831b8014356a71863c977d93d38025f8276687ce87840370ce0db62bde4bfc0" :answer/id #uuid "4d459b3d-ee43-559a-bb76-a63004876263" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126521/selection/2237d24998aa804e6fcc02ee2465fcd2d0b702c3a2446f309580f9d4ee3eba25" :answer/id #uuid "d456a18e-7bbd-5d16-a6bc-92686e035fb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126521/selection/f0a232b43fd658bd5f01e2f21a0b8c169fa81ced689b23567464248da1c83011" :answer/id #uuid "98131914-04d2-536a-b150-2b199d6772ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126521/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a46e88b-c202-5eae-9589-8b035ce9dd67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-126521/selection/2237d24998aa804e6fcc02ee2465fcd2d0b702c3a2446f309580f9d4ee3eba25" :answer-field/choices ["q-126521/selection/03b2af20ed1e2ea358d70c5f4f3b7e7bfd35970c4c6194648dba88c96215de87" "q-126521/selection/1ea68ea8aca2efd79850eb2489b3463c6afabdb3cd1b94855d0dbf435a934118" "q-126521/selection/5831b8014356a71863c977d93d38025f8276687ce87840370ce0db62bde4bfc0" "q-126521/selection/2237d24998aa804e6fcc02ee2465fcd2d0b702c3a2446f309580f9d4ee3eba25" "q-126521/selection/f0a232b43fd658bd5f01e2f21a0b8c169fa81ced689b23567464248da1c83011"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126521" :question/id #uuid "c8299664-2eac-54b1-a2d8-b4c54e28a46f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126521" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\sqrt{1+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-126521/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126521"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109126/selection/30376a42b8f2b1bbc10b61c208b573892f31fec19f978345630628b8a4733535" :answer/id #uuid "9ea1d500-c4f5-5588-9f66-4ed34cdba364" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109126/selection/e0e1d5d491da413959253481874c6c737aacb39c2d3aff20d4478597ae4ebe0e" :answer/id #uuid "ef9bb627-c3d3-5218-9b65-2e009e2d03f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109126/selection/1e51f965461b49ca364c44c307cad4e6a51eab802857977d6ea616111fae60a1" :answer/id #uuid "2d48d9c0-2fd6-52ed-a63d-b4280c603f41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109126/selection/6bc659a854d0a06b1ab9d2fc3fc852c1c8e24af71130f98d95fb9ae98168efa3" :answer/id #uuid "809a2df8-f0bb-5c25-b7cb-cdcd7c6e6926" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109126/selection/835eaca627cacff00a9cc1bf38d0c0ae85fd46afccb549be7d910916c3653e14" :answer/id #uuid "4c8ef166-260e-5b93-a31a-1fa7c86fe533" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109126/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3ac69c48-690b-5dbb-90d9-c7fef8cfbcfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109126/selection/6bc659a854d0a06b1ab9d2fc3fc852c1c8e24af71130f98d95fb9ae98168efa3" :answer-field/choices ["q-109126/selection/30376a42b8f2b1bbc10b61c208b573892f31fec19f978345630628b8a4733535" "q-109126/selection/e0e1d5d491da413959253481874c6c737aacb39c2d3aff20d4478597ae4ebe0e" "q-109126/selection/1e51f965461b49ca364c44c307cad4e6a51eab802857977d6ea616111fae60a1" "q-109126/selection/6bc659a854d0a06b1ab9d2fc3fc852c1c8e24af71130f98d95fb9ae98168efa3" "q-109126/selection/835eaca627cacff00a9cc1bf38d0c0ae85fd46afccb549be7d910916c3653e14"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109126" :question/id #uuid "d3725a9b-dcb4-5fa2-b792-1fe048d5722b" :question/math-academy-id "q-109126" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/0edb5218c2d1527957053a35e992c7538a8857132a6d48114e16e5f71d3848d6.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ that lies in the first quadrant between the line $x=0$ and its $x$-intercept. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-109126/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109126"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109143/selection/bf009cf6518651be4fba417d5043c4177d6b221d72b62271a80d213098e93fb9" :answer/id #uuid "ac831a82-a090-54d6-9010-ffc5d03e7366" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109143/selection/f0423b88ddda799fa83d61b81b6286f630e319bf69839727f44edc6f2ef5fdc7" :answer/id #uuid "d80f9add-3302-57c0-ab4c-9a7dd64c9b39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109143/selection/1053b5c4a33c575e1b158339b7f8376f69057702938264b73998fe37d62afcc6" :answer/id #uuid "fb2fc180-a898-5391-a02c-2840c3930a52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109143/selection/e9431019dc85f27cc3bf3a095fe1183de6afc1e83fcd7c11391159e959a46a35" :answer/id #uuid "df7ee050-ac27-587b-8a1c-e8f6c2330af9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109143/selection/5c3efc1e47799e1aea1983e7b18286108c69d8cce193640964520ec50166bf54" :answer/id #uuid "c0d5ff1c-5418-58fd-9cc2-c5fe364627fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{(2-x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109143/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "99b52f2f-571f-5ea6-b5a1-79051e19fdf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109143/selection/f0423b88ddda799fa83d61b81b6286f630e319bf69839727f44edc6f2ef5fdc7" :answer-field/choices ["q-109143/selection/bf009cf6518651be4fba417d5043c4177d6b221d72b62271a80d213098e93fb9" "q-109143/selection/f0423b88ddda799fa83d61b81b6286f630e319bf69839727f44edc6f2ef5fdc7" "q-109143/selection/1053b5c4a33c575e1b158339b7f8376f69057702938264b73998fe37d62afcc6" "q-109143/selection/e9431019dc85f27cc3bf3a095fe1183de6afc1e83fcd7c11391159e959a46a35" "q-109143/selection/5c3efc1e47799e1aea1983e7b18286108c69d8cce193640964520ec50166bf54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109143" :question/id #uuid "95f65cb5-fc27-5c03-8504-7e25fce5f434" :question/math-academy-id "q-109143" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=2-\\sqrt{x}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=4.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2-\\sqrt{x})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-109143/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109143"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109123/selection/41a589383802c8d783a9a98b2382e8bdb1d828a7227e9474750e9ae08f1a0bf9" :answer/id #uuid "7a26fcb7-dd8a-5338-afc8-d3346caf5af3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109123/selection/fbe98b6a0949b2fb8e2db9c2ecb0fcb79bde35a715e8ca022e91c7453bd4a68d" :answer/id #uuid "43f8de21-5132-56dc-b00e-16bcc25ac974" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109123/selection/d3af307f96a18f5815958f66d52077b5520f36ec01179bf447375054da37040c" :answer/id #uuid "73c71bf5-78f3-503c-a5c2-9b1777e83a99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(x+1)^{2}\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109123/selection/efd0be725da523cb6bbac1f73df3e4b73d15bef51c4f6cbc7ae5370017e6dbfd" :answer/id #uuid "6d2363e5-fed2-505d-a7b3-cfd6f73c84f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-x-1)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+5x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109123/selection/8b4825c9596ea242c96ada9b7ceb1e4346e56c0c28fc908f46567438744dd569" :answer/id #uuid "a141414b-dd92-556c-8029-8f7e488e8a14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+5x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109123/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2b847f4-9c39-5384-8695-2e0e2e99e7bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109123/selection/41a589383802c8d783a9a98b2382e8bdb1d828a7227e9474750e9ae08f1a0bf9" :answer-field/choices ["q-109123/selection/41a589383802c8d783a9a98b2382e8bdb1d828a7227e9474750e9ae08f1a0bf9" "q-109123/selection/fbe98b6a0949b2fb8e2db9c2ecb0fcb79bde35a715e8ca022e91c7453bd4a68d" "q-109123/selection/d3af307f96a18f5815958f66d52077b5520f36ec01179bf447375054da37040c" "q-109123/selection/efd0be725da523cb6bbac1f73df3e4b73d15bef51c4f6cbc7ae5370017e6dbfd" "q-109123/selection/8b4825c9596ea242c96ada9b7ceb1e4346e56c0c28fc908f46567438744dd569"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109123" :question/id #uuid "caf996b3-98c2-50cc-98cb-eb0ffa63955b" :question/math-academy-id "q-109123" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/924444fa2da1709d788d3b3522e86743b46ee1c5a25999bb77731449e621a07d.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=1-(x+1)^{2}$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(x+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-1\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-2,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-(x+1)^{2})=-2x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(1-(x+1)^{2})\\sqrt{1+(-2x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-2x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+8x+5}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-109123/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109123"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109124/selection/b2a311e627e3b0302988dd61f3386a908e13643c935b66edd953e212a9583c4f" :answer/id #uuid "53ec25e1-d3f1-5a34-a28e-65a51c59f377" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109124/selection/c35138ea022a490f00fdcebfefe32ab0f10f72fa7bbea12fd909e2ebbda35236" :answer/id #uuid "48b4df15-19e4-5784-b061-e31b571bf95f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109124/selection/4dab9eea3ad3a3a8915168799fab063d3a7cba322f1aa7974db1c28792a931bc" :answer/id #uuid "55435d40-60df-5029-9541-d3418b01a072" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109124/selection/6c302304d4a35732abf3a37f4b6301ca2e9413cb08cb3701c64c4ae19fd296f4" :answer/id #uuid "f18e0084-a327-5799-8f69-0abf48158eaf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109124/selection/32d7d19f6ae355a1838dd13c6d7865d8f9254defca677a696ff1b5ab39ce76fe" :answer/id #uuid "cd33f6bc-be76-5811-acf9-eac8fbeec2e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109124/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8cbff233-1b51-5b4b-add4-c165709eaaa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109124/selection/c35138ea022a490f00fdcebfefe32ab0f10f72fa7bbea12fd909e2ebbda35236" :answer-field/choices ["q-109124/selection/b2a311e627e3b0302988dd61f3386a908e13643c935b66edd953e212a9583c4f" "q-109124/selection/c35138ea022a490f00fdcebfefe32ab0f10f72fa7bbea12fd909e2ebbda35236" "q-109124/selection/4dab9eea3ad3a3a8915168799fab063d3a7cba322f1aa7974db1c28792a931bc" "q-109124/selection/6c302304d4a35732abf3a37f4b6301ca2e9413cb08cb3701c64c4ae19fd296f4" "q-109124/selection/32d7d19f6ae355a1838dd13c6d7865d8f9254defca677a696ff1b5ab39ce76fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109124" :question/id #uuid "630e6917-1b31-5ed2-909b-732e3490f5cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-109124" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=1$ and $x=e.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=1$ to ${x}_{2}=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-109124/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109124"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109155/selection/ea392aed874d8b0352a13f5c337a5c2bba04af32ec129ee92dddd0ddbe220a08" :answer/id #uuid "2f29ee2e-c0e3-59ac-9766-f0fd265878bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109155/selection/fd4dd47817694ac9e68c410b947dfa6aaceed7c1ebefba1e5b6fac3562b0b34c" :answer/id #uuid "f86696a7-7152-54f4-b6f6-64a05f3e2976" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109155/selection/3ad5ad1b4d45c15f70dfae190ebdc06af9a77de82cae68806f300b2cc17fa805" :answer/id #uuid "ff5ee6ab-b700-5c7a-8309-f7bc7b0e046a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109155/selection/078c5c7910730671b00375aa58c87450e672ea499704ac6c477fa604847a515f" :answer/id #uuid "c3846b18-068f-5d46-b8e9-ae330a812c0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109155/selection/74969f4fe56c65e8e8a26fe04c3a62732e6df1b05830de00104ea342e0da7956" :answer/id #uuid "cd0482db-620c-5115-9d37-2e8fd660b699" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109155/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d9b2d52e-608f-56ca-b7c9-86ab9b88ad0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109155/selection/3ad5ad1b4d45c15f70dfae190ebdc06af9a77de82cae68806f300b2cc17fa805" :answer-field/choices ["q-109155/selection/ea392aed874d8b0352a13f5c337a5c2bba04af32ec129ee92dddd0ddbe220a08" "q-109155/selection/fd4dd47817694ac9e68c410b947dfa6aaceed7c1ebefba1e5b6fac3562b0b34c" "q-109155/selection/3ad5ad1b4d45c15f70dfae190ebdc06af9a77de82cae68806f300b2cc17fa805" "q-109155/selection/078c5c7910730671b00375aa58c87450e672ea499704ac6c477fa604847a515f" "q-109155/selection/74969f4fe56c65e8e8a26fe04c3a62732e6df1b05830de00104ea342e0da7956"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109155" :question/id #uuid "52c76f08-0943-5c3e-9636-b2f7eab362d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-109155" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/22bb16a874005f09d5cbad055f149f07e2139f559a2a04368c32696567714b26.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}$ that lies in the first quadrant between the line $y=0$ and its $y$-intercept. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-109155/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109155"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-104533"] :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡y$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)={sec}^{2}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{({sec}^{2}⁡y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-109188/selection/cc803c4802e1e1f1cc785a0da57e0e5855c23ddb2b85480df9474d387faaca02" :answer/id #uuid "d94a6cfb-ed38-5eb5-9db9-c191cb50a173" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{e}y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109188/selection/94cdab7be785a2a046c7c024f7610dda173beb1bdfd0fd2a3a0b05191298d749" :answer/id #uuid "922c0fe5-a954-57dd-9c66-c82340b63378" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109188/selection/c5940a57edf938d8a5b9ac0e6c1f4e7ebd7984255511308fbbfef87d8db8375c" :answer/id #uuid "b8214ee2-7c44-59c9-b036-88a88600709a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109188/selection/ce54a05e0a387851c0ddb237123e22d78c79d490f5d7e693b44a0438d06c7e25" :answer/id #uuid "60cc550d-4716-5c87-8ade-1c3b3b9567f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109188/selection/d7ed06785c364bfc030a2943fcee2991cff8574d2ebe9e92525d35d1efe73bba" :answer/id #uuid "9d8c08f0-c24d-5981-89cf-b253b8083cb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{y({y}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109188/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "93548155-9136-514c-9ff4-2ad626f0b8d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109188/selection/ce54a05e0a387851c0ddb237123e22d78c79d490f5d7e693b44a0438d06c7e25" :answer-field/choices ["q-109188/selection/cc803c4802e1e1f1cc785a0da57e0e5855c23ddb2b85480df9474d387faaca02" "q-109188/selection/94cdab7be785a2a046c7c024f7610dda173beb1bdfd0fd2a3a0b05191298d749" "q-109188/selection/c5940a57edf938d8a5b9ac0e6c1f4e7ebd7984255511308fbbfef87d8db8375c" "q-109188/selection/ce54a05e0a387851c0ddb237123e22d78c79d490f5d7e693b44a0438d06c7e25" "q-109188/selection/d7ed06785c364bfc030a2943fcee2991cff8574d2ebe9e92525d35d1efe73bba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109188" :question/id #uuid "6cb738bd-1657-50aa-acee-1f912676f25f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109188" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/9b27f44aa7ef6bef95a88ccfcb6495d17e9457a7452bf602f87dcd5b95ec71dc.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercept and the line $y=e.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=1$ to $y=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-109188/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109188"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-109179"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/dc94ee46e2b9381afb6f62b74c67d76d137f857c3f78f36df2fdad5661b114b3.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=2y-{y}^{3}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(2-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y-{y}^{3})=2-3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(2-3{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{9{y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-126551/selection/55827cc4ee581021e93c5d00826001aa52f55bd97891aae2401fdeca25125c3d" :answer/id #uuid "0c157c29-07e2-5505-aefe-3127849fc32f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126551/selection/faa77295a151bf53a1d8c2b3ca464ad5816542b4c3c117588323a6bfec134b93" :answer/id #uuid "3aa65cf1-8862-5e81-9eb0-646b769e8f3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-2y)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126551/selection/6073de8a15f1df53eb594df5d80ce117533771ca3ae7a3a9392123a2e33820f5" :answer/id #uuid "c14f66cb-598d-53b3-a7d9-7a82afc56072" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126551/selection/e51f5137e9fdc345a3d1f965624e8f2dbe9d33c2c8b88d666b8f4e30ad2dab39" :answer/id #uuid "bdffeeda-8453-5f19-94c8-d471dbddd8ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-2y)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126551/selection/e38ae282f90678b53e4d3cf4982f5251f41c500e3c3639306f7f9a39ca9fd61d" :answer/id #uuid "f48ceb38-e9d5-5645-9034-4ddd4d3c1763" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126551/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2cb6d9f6-2140-5991-b864-4fea6b588ca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-126551/selection/55827cc4ee581021e93c5d00826001aa52f55bd97891aae2401fdeca25125c3d" :answer-field/choices ["q-126551/selection/55827cc4ee581021e93c5d00826001aa52f55bd97891aae2401fdeca25125c3d" "q-126551/selection/faa77295a151bf53a1d8c2b3ca464ad5816542b4c3c117588323a6bfec134b93" "q-126551/selection/6073de8a15f1df53eb594df5d80ce117533771ca3ae7a3a9392123a2e33820f5" "q-126551/selection/e51f5137e9fdc345a3d1f965624e8f2dbe9d33c2c8b88d666b8f4e30ad2dab39" "q-126551/selection/e38ae282f90678b53e4d3cf4982f5251f41c500e3c3639306f7f9a39ca9fd61d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126551" :question/id #uuid "01d978c2-8b03-5808-9b4a-ab2bf7c73b35" :question/math-academy-id "q-126551" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/1204968a7d4eee9f299b432c21441f670c7ebfab0629da6ef66a7960033a9c1b.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x={y}^{2}-2y$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ y(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}-2y)=2y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+{(2y-2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-8y+5}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126551/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126551"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353403/field-1/50f1ed9433a59880224f3524b079349462fa2940fafb4dcf786df41d47dbc521" :answer/id #uuid "753d94e0-a2a2-549f-8dc0-d6b142b11e2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{49\\pi}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353403/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "83bd1d8e-494a-50ce-a109-f8a42a88b9d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353403/field-1/50f1ed9433a59880224f3524b079349462fa2940fafb4dcf786df41d47dbc521" :answer-field/choices ["q-353403/field-1/50f1ed9433a59880224f3524b079349462fa2940fafb4dcf786df41d47dbc521"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353403" :question/id #uuid "a2419aff-382b-524d-8eee-9f4042d5d08e" :question/math-academy-id "q-353403" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{3x+1}$ that lies between the lines $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x=1.$ Find the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3x+1})=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+1}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4(3x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{3x+1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4(3x+1)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{3x+1}\\sqrt{\\frac{12x+13}{4(3x+1)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{12x+13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/3}^{1}\\sqrt{12x+13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot \\frac{(12x+13)^{3/2}}{18}\\,{|}_{-1/3}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{18}({25}^{3/2}-{9}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{49\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-353403/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353403"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-126589/selection/9176f349afd3e388ad5158410b09fa4a142428a97238f1dbccb6fdf1e075588e" :answer/id #uuid "dc98e8cd-9d57-5de2-89b9-78853d7a23cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126589/selection/3e00f2e382a8ed7987661a9fafda289263980878d64628bd566b4fb5ad04eb3a" :answer/id #uuid "0c828721-be3c-5849-8600-f8b2e58afe67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126589/selection/c74fea088fe0714efaa2974c4a6784f3570cc17c02518e027ce90925d59ea0ad" :answer/id #uuid "c4028995-1484-5909-9581-c2d66f900e96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126589/selection/a116f3810a10b1a22686a143e31bc9a6b030d64cf25cadef00b02e48a4257275" :answer/id #uuid "9fe596ad-226e-55fa-a4f4-39c8f3e379f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi (\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126589/selection/c4198e2858556b305e847671093ea2fef85cb38be58c5a93829ece7b0320794e" :answer/id #uuid "a5d3ed15-fc6a-558c-b1f1-13345a642e17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi (3\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126589/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "09769ad5-a9f0-5417-8bf1-c063e61e33a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-126589/selection/9176f349afd3e388ad5158410b09fa4a142428a97238f1dbccb6fdf1e075588e" :answer-field/choices ["q-126589/selection/9176f349afd3e388ad5158410b09fa4a142428a97238f1dbccb6fdf1e075588e" "q-126589/selection/3e00f2e382a8ed7987661a9fafda289263980878d64628bd566b4fb5ad04eb3a" "q-126589/selection/c74fea088fe0714efaa2974c4a6784f3570cc17c02518e027ce90925d59ea0ad" "q-126589/selection/a116f3810a10b1a22686a143e31bc9a6b030d64cf25cadef00b02e48a4257275" "q-126589/selection/c4198e2858556b305e847671093ea2fef85cb38be58c5a93829ece7b0320794e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126589" :question/id #uuid "89554c02-d315-5a8d-8464-ca9cd53eae70" :question/math-academy-id "q-126589" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1)=y\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu={y}^{2}+1\\,⇒\\,(\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u=y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\frac{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}{3}\\,|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}(\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-126589/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126589"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353405/field-1/9ededd469b677457e439cd2c0206191738674b0924069a1926fbc586ed135328" :answer/id #uuid "89fec85d-f8a0-5718-825e-7f130d1db9e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353405/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7036810-ada4-5374-a85e-4c88fa0fa505" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353405/field-1/9ededd469b677457e439cd2c0206191738674b0924069a1926fbc586ed135328" :answer-field/choices ["q-353405/field-1/9ededd469b677457e439cd2c0206191738674b0924069a1926fbc586ed135328"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353405" :question/id #uuid "45434f1e-aeeb-591d-8147-87710b9c595b" :question/math-academy-id "q-353405" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=2x+1$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ Find the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1)=2\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\sqrt{1+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}({x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}((1+1)-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-353405/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353405"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109319/selection/e7418f9b7a8bed63589af55c006da41ff32ee2049206989344f38152403733a8" :answer/id #uuid "73d6c78a-807e-566c-8a7b-820218a705df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109319/selection/b93de945d9fa631f3ba6215bb7d05f2400ae11535465e80a61be24e3a197de85" :answer/id #uuid "3bf3ffbc-d7e5-582c-9ce3-204ddbd465f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}(2-\\sqrt{5})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109319/selection/9ed588e66db4dad769e22677e51a7bb730248cb44b3393998a779a74a77c8287" :answer/id #uuid "3f407bcc-8ed3-5d07-8aa0-85b60cd8bcc3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(5-\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109319/selection/6c1c76a33f143b009d67baf2e135b55e8b667b2018c2cebff216fa7e887cd182" :answer/id #uuid "a227ddfa-8f31-529a-aced-ec8333463edf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi (3-\\sqrt{5})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109319/selection/b7a981be185bd81da54fb625b267c02396f74f1b3a076dbe7550e3ea53a567e1" :answer/id #uuid "3f79674a-5183-5d7b-9737-fbfcf90030ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi (6-\\sqrt{5})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109319/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4306813d-1d6e-5b41-aec2-235f501e995a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109319/selection/6c1c76a33f143b009d67baf2e135b55e8b667b2018c2cebff216fa7e887cd182" :answer-field/choices ["q-109319/selection/e7418f9b7a8bed63589af55c006da41ff32ee2049206989344f38152403733a8" "q-109319/selection/b93de945d9fa631f3ba6215bb7d05f2400ae11535465e80a61be24e3a197de85" "q-109319/selection/9ed588e66db4dad769e22677e51a7bb730248cb44b3393998a779a74a77c8287" "q-109319/selection/6c1c76a33f143b009d67baf2e135b55e8b667b2018c2cebff216fa7e887cd182" "q-109319/selection/b7a981be185bd81da54fb625b267c02396f74f1b3a076dbe7550e3ea53a567e1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109319" :question/id #uuid "777aa170-0dc9-512e-8f03-a8a705e1c469" :question/math-academy-id "q-109319" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=9-{y}^{2}\\,⇒\\,\\text{d}u=-2y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3\\pi {\\int }_{9}^{5}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{9}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\pi {[2\\sqrt{u}\\,]}_{5}^{9} \\\\  & =6\\pi (3-\\sqrt{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-109319/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109319"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353411/field-1/dbecedde29307c965cc7d1481e060b17cd840c9e713d3f79d972e07a9f1cddc6" :answer/id #uuid "7bd5631d-1ba6-5ff0-913d-bd4ab5adf0f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{149\\pi}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353411/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a1e728d-36c5-5ec0-987c-1723f9717512" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353411/field-1/dbecedde29307c965cc7d1481e060b17cd840c9e713d3f79d972e07a9f1cddc6" :answer-field/choices ["q-353411/field-1/dbecedde29307c965cc7d1481e060b17cd840c9e713d3f79d972e07a9f1cddc6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353411" :question/id #uuid "83dd7e58-e9f5-5d83-b520-e7682449df98" :question/math-academy-id "q-353411" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Calculate the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3/2})=2\\sqrt{y}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+4y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=1+4y\\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}y,\\,y=\\frac{u-1}{4},\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+4y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{u-1}{4}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}(u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})\\,{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}((\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {9}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {9}^{3/2})-(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {1}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}((\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot 243-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 27)-(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}(\\frac{396}{5}+\\frac{4}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{149\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-353411/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353411"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8905"] :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=e^x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=0$ to $x_2={2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1+({e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9120"] :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^3-y$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082610/assets/1bdbe21cbb517645dfe9362f3313c2d85118acfea1d839fe33ae9b9ac6c3ed50.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}-y)=3{y}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{(3{y}^{2}-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{2-6{y}^{2}+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8909"] :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ that lies between the lines $x=2$ and $x=4.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=2$ to $x_2=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x-2})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{\\frac{4x-7}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-7}}{2\\sqrt{x-2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=4x-7 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm du = 4\\,\\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
